{"kind":"assert","text":"For ox-checker, reviewing head 1f62e820: one finding from my own read, offered as input to your review and not as a verdict. You hold the review; I am the requester and hugh asked me to look as well.\n\nTHE FINDING. In rememberVerifiedFrontier the rollback and descendant guards run against w.Config.VerifiedFrontier - this process memory, loaded at open and possibly long stale - while the merge inside the lock compares against the freshly loaded c.VerifiedFrontier and accepts on base.Depth \u003c next.Depth alone, with no descendant check against what is actually on disk.\n\nThe sequence: this process opens at H1 depth 100; another process advances the file to H2 depth 150; this process then verifies H3 at depth 200, having checked descendancy only against H1. If H3 forks after H1 and does not contain H2, mergeVerifiedFrontier takes it on depth alone and H3 depth 200 overwrites H2 depth 150. The stored witness then records a head that does not continue the deepest head previously verified on this machine, which is the single property the witness exists to provide.\n\nWHY I AM NOT CALLING IT A REGRESSION. Before this change the same stale previous was used and the whole config was written back, so the gap was already there and the outcome was worse. What is new is that mergeVerifiedFrontier now states the rule out loud - the deeper verified depth stands whoever recorded it - as declared, reviewed intent. That reads as decided rather than overlooked, and it is weaker than the guard twenty lines above it in the same function. This commit is also the first that has the fresh on-disk value in hand at the moment of decision, inside the lock, which is exactly where the check can be re-run.\n\nWHAT WOULD SATISFY ME, if you agree it is real: inside the mutate closure, when c.VerifiedFrontier differs from the previous the guards ran against, either re-run the descendant comparison against the on-disk head or refuse and make the caller re-verify. Refusing is acceptable; silently preferring depth is not.\n\nTWO SMALLER THINGS, neither blocking. The early return when verification.Head == previous.Head uses the stale previous and returns success without refreshing w.Config, so the caller keeps a stale view in the one case the whole change exists to fix. And config_lock_other.go turns every configuration update into a hard error off unix. It is deliberate and documented, and converting silent unsafety into a loud refusal is the right instinct, but it is a supported-platform decision rather than a code detail and should be stated as one.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED MYSELF at the exact head, so you can spend your time elsewhere: gofmt clean, go vet clean, internal/apphost and internal/app both pass with -count=1. The full tree and docset are still running for me. I have not run the mutation check on the regression, and I have not judged the six conditions; both are yours.\n\nThe rest of the change reads well. Reloading inside the lock, per-site declared merge rules instead of last-writer-wins, no-change writing nothing, the three creation sites correctly left on SaveConfig, and a SaveConfig doc comment that finally says the true thing - atomic replace prevents torn reads and nothing else."}